+# Origin migration
+
+The installed app moves from `https://dailygrind.example.com` (old) to
+`https://app.dailygrind.example.com` (new). Both origins are same-site
+(`example.com`), which Web App Origin Migration requires.
+
+| Where | File | Purpose |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| New origin (repo root) | `manifest.webmanifest` | `id: "/"` + `migrate_from` naming the old app's manifest ID |
+| New origin | `migration.js` | Copies `dg:*` localStorage settings from the old origin via a hidden iframe + `postMessage` |
+| Old origin (`old-origin/`) | `.well-known/web-app-origin-association` | Authorizes migration to `https://app.dailygrind.example.com/` |
+| Old origin | `manifest.webmanifest` | `migrate_to` so already-installed users get offered the update without visiting the new site |
+| Old origin | `migration-bridge.html` | Sends saved settings to the new origin only (`targetOrigin` restricted) |
+| Old origin | `index.html` | "We've moved" banner for Firefox/Safari, which don't support migration |
+
+Notes:
+- Only the installation (OS shortcuts, home-screen/taskbar icons) migrates. Storage, cookies and
+  permissions stay with the origin, so settings get copied by `migration.js`; permissions have to be granted again.
+- Set `behavior` to `"force"` in `migrate_from` to make the update mandatory (the user picks either Update or Remove).
+- Serve `old-origin/.well-known/web-app-origin-association` as `application/json` and make sure
+  the host doesn't hide dot-directories.
+- Store new user preferences under keys that start with `dg:` so they can be migrated.
