@@ -0,0 +1,48 @@
+# My Account — Passkeys
+
+The homepage's **My Account** button opens a modal `<dialog>` (`index.html`) driven by `account.js`.
+`/#account` opens it directly.
+
+| Feature | Where |
+| --- | --- |
+| Sign in (passkey autofill via conditional mediation + "Sign in with passkey" button) | signed-out view |
+| Create account with a passkey | signed-out view |
+| List / rename / delete passkeys, create another passkey | signed-in view |
+| Update name + email (synced to password managers) | signed-in view |
+| Reauthentication with the user's own passkeys before deleting a passkey or changing the profile | signed-in view |
+
+The client calls the native WebAuthn APIs directly. `vendor/webauthn-polyfills-0.1.7.js` is an esbuild
+bundle of [`webauthn-polyfills@0.1.7`](https://github.com/passkeydeveloper/webauthn-polyfills) (Apache-2.0),
+served from our own origin. It fills in `getClientCapabilities()`, `parse*OptionsFromJSON()` and
+`toJSON()` on browsers that don't have them yet.
+
+## Backend contract
+
+All endpoints are same-origin JSON and use a cookie session. Errors return `{ "error": "message" }`,
+and the UI shows that message. Use a vetted server library to generate options and verify responses,
+e.g. [SimpleWebAuthn](https://github.com/MasterKale/SimpleWebAuthn) (`@simplewebauthn/server`).
+
+`RP_ID` is a server constant. The client never derives it from `location.hostname`. Credential IDs and
+`userId` must be unpadded Base64URL strings.
+
+| Method & path | Request | Response |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `GET /api/session` | — | `200 { user: { name, displayName } }` or `401` |
+| `POST /api/signin/options` | `{}` | Request options JSON: `rpId`, `challenge`, `allowCredentials: []`, `userVerification: "preferred"`. Store the challenge and UV level in the session. |
+| `POST /api/signin/verify` | `credential.toJSON()` | `200 { user }` and start the session. `404` if the credential ID is unknown, so the client can call `signalUnknownCredential`. Update `lastUsedAt` and `counter`. |
+| `POST /api/signup/options` | `{ name, displayName }` | Creation options JSON with `rp.id`, a new Base64URL `user.id`, `residentKey: "required"`, `requireResidentKey: true`, `userVerification: "preferred"`, `excludeCredentials`. Keep the pending account in the session. |
+| `POST /api/signup/verify` | `credential.toJSON()` | Create the user and the credential, start the session → `200 { user }`. Use `requireUserVerification: false` when UV was `preferred`. |
+| `POST /api/passkeys/options` | `{}` (auth) | Creation options for the signed-in user. Don't set `authenticatorAttachment`, so security keys work. Fill `excludeCredentials` with the user's existing passkeys. |
+| `POST /api/passkeys/verify` | `credential.toJSON()` (auth) | Store the credential. Derive `name` and `providerIcon` from the AAGUID registry (or "Unknown passkey provider" for the all-zero AAGUID). → `200 {}` |
+| `GET /api/passkeys` | — (auth) | `{ rpId, userId, credentials: [{ id, name, providerIcon?, credentialDeviceType, credentialBackedUp, registeredAt, lastUsedAt? }] }` (epoch ms) |
+| `PUT /api/passkeys/:id` | `{ name }` (auth) | Updated credential. `404` if it isn't the user's credential. |
+| `DELETE /api/passkeys/:id` | — (auth, **recent reauth**) | `200 { success: true }`. `404` if it isn't the user's credential. |
+| `PUT /api/user` | `{ name, displayName }` (auth, **recent reauth**) | `{ name, displayName, rpId, userId }` |
+| `POST /api/reauth/options` | `{}` (auth) | Request options with `allowCredentials` listing **only** the session user's credentials (and their transports). |
+| `POST /api/reauth/verify` | `credential.toJSON()` (auth) | Reject unless the credential's `passkeyUserId` equals the session user. Record the reauth time (e.g. it is valid for 5 minutes) → `200 {}`. `404` for unknown credentials. |
+| `POST /api/signout` | `{}` | End the session. |
+
+The server must enforce "recent reauth" on the delete and profile endpoints itself. The client
+always reauthenticates first, but that check alone isn't enough.
+
+If passkeys can't be created on the device, the UI links to `/login` for a password sign-in.
