+        <p>Every bag of coffee we roast begins months before it reaches our drum, on hillsides most of our customers
+          will never see. Long before we talk about flavor notes or brew ratios, we talk about people: the farmers,
+          pickers, and mill operators whose work determines whether a coffee will be ordinary or unforgettable. Our
+          sourcing process is built around one simple idea: if we take care of the people who grow the coffee, the
+          coffee takes care of itself.</p>
+
+        <h3>Starting with relationships, not auctions</h3>
+        <p>We buy the majority of our green coffee directly from a small group of producers in Colombia, Ethiopia,
+          Guatemala, and Sumatra. Some of these partnerships are now entering their eighth year. Rather than chasing
+          whatever lot scores highest on a given cupping table, we commit to the same farms season after season. That
+          consistency lets growers plan ahead, invest in their land, and experiment with new processing methods
+          without worrying about whether a buyer will still be there next harvest.</p>
+        <p>Each year, at least one member of our roasting team travels to origin during harvest. We walk the rows,
+          eat with the families we work with, and talk openly about what went well and what didn't. These visits are
+          less about inspection and more about listening. Many of our best ideas, from adjusting drying times to
+          trying a honey process on a single terrace, have come from conversations held on a farmhouse porch.</p>
+
+        <h3>Paying for quality, and paying fairly</h3>
+        <p>We pay well above Fairtrade minimums for every coffee we purchase, and we publish the price we paid per
+          pound alongside each single-origin offering. Our pricing is tied to cup quality rather than the commodity
+          market, which means a sudden drop in futures prices doesn't translate into a pay cut for the farmers who
+          grew an exceptional lot. When a producer reaches a higher quality tier, they receive a premium that is
+          agreed upon before the harvest begins, not negotiated afterward.</p>
+        <p>We also pre-finance a portion of each harvest. Paying part of the contract up front helps families cover
+          the cost of labor and equipment during the months when money is tightest, and it removes the pressure to
+          sell cherries early to local middlemen at a discount.</p>
+
